All four traces (mpi, mpi-openmp, cuda-mpi, mpi-kokkos-openmp) have been recorded with evidence-backed assertions covering parallelism model, communication patterns, memory characteristics, accelerator usage, and I/O behavior. Summary of findings:

**miniFE** (Mantevo mini-application for unstructured implicit FE codes) builds a `miniFE.x` executable across several variants sharing the same algorithmic core (3D box partition → FE assembly into sparse matrix → CG solve), but differing in parallelization backend:

1. **`mpi`** (ref/): pure MPI, point-to-point neighbor halo exchange (`MPI_Send`/`MPI_Irecv`) plus `MPI_Allreduce` for CG dot products — the classic sparse-CG communication signature (neighbor + reduction).

2. **`mpi-openmp`** (openmp/): same MPI pattern, with `#pragma omp parallel for` added to AXPY/dot/matvec/assembly kernels for intra-node threading.

3. **`cuda-mpi`** (cuda/): CUDA device kernels (thrust, ELL sparse matrix format) per rank, MPI halo exchange staged through pinned host buffers via `cudaMemcpyAsync`, with an optional `GPUDIRECT` build flag for direct device-to-device MPI transfers, and `MATVEC_OVERLAP` to overlap communication with local compute.

4. **`mpi-kokkos-openmp`** (kokkos/): same MPI communication structure, computational kernels expressed through Kokkos (default Makefile targets OpenMP backend, though Cuda backend is possible via Kokkos build config).

All variants are consistently memory-bandwidth bound with very low arithmetic intensity (streaming sparse-matvec/dot-product kernels), no checkpoint/restart, and no parallel I/O beyond a final YAML report file.
